      <h2>What trauma work looks like here</h2>
      <p>Nothing gets opened faster than you can stay present for. That is not caution for its
      own sake: a nervous system that floods learns that attention to the body is dangerous,
      and then the work gets harder rather than easier.</p>
      <p>You do not have to tell the whole story. Somatic Experiencing tracks what the body is
      still holding rather than what the mind can narrate, and NARM asks where a pattern was
      learned and whether it is still serving you, rather than excavating the past for its own
      sake.</p>
      <p>With developmental trauma we are usually building capacity rather than processing a
      single event, so the arc is longer and the review is done together instead of guessed at
      the start.</p>
      <h3>What people bring here</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Patterns that formed early, before there were words or choices for them.</li>
        <li>C-PTSD and PTSD, including trauma that has not finished happening.</li>
        <li>Understanding your history perfectly well and nothing changing.</li>
        <li>Going numb, or flooding, whenever you approach the hard material.</li>
        <li>Self-criticism doing most of the talking.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>If EMDR has stalled before</h3>
      <p>The usual story: preparation looked adequate, processing started, and then it flooded,
      flattened or went nowhere. People tend to read that as proof the memory is untouchable.
      It is more often a sign that something in you was never asked.</p>
      <p>Most of the work here happens before any processing does, which is why people who have
      tried EMDR elsewhere end up on this page.
      <a href="/approach/ifs-informed-emdr/">How IFS-informed EMDR handles the part that
      objects</a>.</p>
