<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about trauma therapy</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What is developmental trauma, and how is it different from PTSD?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>PTSD usually refers to the aftermath of an identifiable event. Developmental trauma is
      what accumulates from the ongoing conditions of early life: attunement that was not there,
      needs that were not safe to have, adaptations that worked then and cost you now. There is
      often no single memory to point at, which is why event-focused protocols alone can miss
      it.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I have to tell you everything that happened?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Somatic Experiencing and NARM both work with what is present now rather than
      requiring a full retelling, and EMDR processing does not need a narrated account either.
      You decide what gets said out loud and when.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How long does trauma therapy take?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It depends what we are working with. Stabilization can be a handful of sessions.
      Developmental trauma is slower, because we are building capacity rather than processing a
      single event. We review the arc together rather than guessing at the start.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>I have tried EMDR before and it stalled. What is different here?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Most stalled EMDR stalls in preparation rather than processing. The IFS-informed
      approach looks for the part of you that is objecting, works out what it needs in place
      first, and only then returns to the target. Reem is an EMDRIA Approved Consultant who
      consults with other therapists on exactly this problem.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do you work with trauma that is still happening?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Ongoing stress and unfinished situations are common here, and the emphasis in
      those cases is stabilization and footing first. Processing work before there is footing
      tends to destabilize rather than help.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I see an associate instead?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes, and often sooner. The practice's LPC Associates work under Reem's supervision, so
      the clinical lineage is the same, at lower fees including sliding scale. Say what is going
      on in the consult and you will be pointed at the right fit.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
